An exception is an object representing an abnormal condition that interrupts the normal flow of a program's execution.

                    Object
                       |
                   Throwable
                  /         \
             Error          Exception
                            /       \
                 RuntimeException    Checked Exceptions
                       |
              NullPointerException
              IllegalArgumentException
              ArithmeticException
              etc.

Exception VS Error:
- Exception represents conditions that an application may reasonably handle.
  Example: IOException, SQLException, IllegalArgumentException, NullPointerException, TimeoutException etc.

-Error represents serious  JVM/system-level problems.
 Example: OutOfMemoryError, StackOverflowError, NoClassDefFoundError, etc.

Note: Errors are not meant to be caught by applications, while exceptions can be caught and handled.


Checked vs Unchecked Exceptions:
- Checked Exceptions: Compiler forces you to handle or declare it
  Example: IOException, SQLException, ClassNotFoundException, etc.
   public void readFile() throwss IOException {    // It throws IOException
       // code that may throw IOException
   }

   or
   try{
        ...
   } catch(){
        ...
   }


- Unchecked Exceptions: Compiler does not force you to handle or declare it. Subclass of RuntimeException
  Example: NullPointerException, IllegalArgumentException, ArithmeticException, etc.

  public void process(int n){
    if(n==null){
        throw new NullPointerException("Input cannot be null");
    }
  }

Throw VS Throws

throw -> action
throws -> declaration

Does finally Always Execute?
    Normally yes, but not absolutely always.
    For example:

        try {
            System.out.println("Hello");
            System.exit(0);
        } finally {
            System.out.println("Finally");
        }

    finally may not execute because the JVM terminates.
    Other extreme cases include:
        JVM crash
        OS termination
        power failure
        native-level failure

    Therefore:
        "finally always executes" is not technically correct.
    Better answer:

        Finally executes during normal control-flow unwinding, except when the JVM/process terminates abruptly or execution cannot continue.